# Purpose

The previous Mukai note proved:

```text
coker K_Mukai ~= Z_7.
```

This note gives the more canonical formulation.

For an integral lattice `P` with Gram matrix `K`, the natural finite group is
not merely a presented cokernel.  It is the discriminant group:

```text
A_P = P^*/P.
```

In a basis of `P`, this group is represented by rational vectors `theta` such
that:

```text
K theta in Z^n,
```

modulo integral vectors.

# Mukai lattice block

Use the positive Mukai vectors:

```text
a=(5,H,0),
b=(7,3H,1),
H^2=2.
```

They span a rank-two integral lattice `P` with Gram matrix:

```text
K_Mukai =
[[2,1],
 [1,4]].
```

Then:

```text
det(K_Mukai)=7,
SNF(K_Mukai)=[7].
```

Therefore:

```text
A_P=P^*/P ~= Z_7.
```

# Explicit generator

An explicit discriminant class is:

```text
theta=(1/7,5/7).
```

It lies in the dual lattice because:

```text
K_Mukai theta = (1,3).
```

It has order seven modulo `P`:

```text
7 theta = (1,5) in P,
j theta notin P for 1<=j<=6.
```

The discriminant pairing gives:

```text
b(theta,theta)=theta^T K_Mukai theta = 2/7 mod 1.
```

So the discriminant form is nontrivial and cyclic of order seven.

# Why this is better

The discriminant-group statement is basis-aware but not basis-arbitrary.  It
depends on the selected integral Mukai sublattice `P`, not on a floating
integer matrix.

Thus the correct odd-sector theorem can be stated as:

```text
A Bianchi-compatible Fu-Yau/K3 topological sector supplies a primitive
positive rank-two Mukai sublattice P.
The family-trivial odd CP labels are the unitary characters of A_P=P^*/P.
Since A_P ~= Z_7, the odd CP factor is Z_7.
```

# What is proved

```text
P has determinant seven.
A_P=P^*/P is cyclic of order seven.
theta=(1/7,5/7) is a generator.
Hom(A_P,U(1)) ~= Z_7.
```

# What remains open

The character-identification theorem is now closed once `A_P` is selected.
The fixed-sector selection reduction is also closed once a sector supplies
`A_P`.  The remaining global theorem is:

```text
Bianchi-compatible Fu-Yau/K3 topological sector
  -> supplied Mukai sublattice P
  -> MTT fixed-sector selection
  -> discriminant group A_P.
```

This is now the most precise form of the `Z_7` proof target.

# Relation to HYM

This still does not claim that `a` and `b` are two HYM summands of one bundle.
They are charge-lattice generators.  The HYM/Fu-Yau anomaly realization remains
a separate physical existence gate.

